***************************************************************** 2831 1451
Aug-2011, BNL, S. Hoblit                                          2831 1451
                                                                  2831 1451
The COMMARA-2.0 covariances were adopted for Ni-60 in the fast    2831 1451
neutron region. Here, like in the Cr and Fe materials, the        2831 1451
starting point was the ENDF/B-VI.8 covariances which resulted     2831 1451
from an evaluation by the highly experienced ORNL group. Then,    2831 1451
these data were subject to a dispersion analysis, leading to      2831 1451
certain modifications of uncertainties, while correlations were   2831 1451
left unchanged.                                                   2831 1451
***************************************************************** 2831 1451
ORNL-  July 2009                                                  2831 1451
                                                                  2831 1451
     Reevaluation of Ni-60 resonance parameters in the energy     2831 1451
range thermal to 810 keV. H. Derrien, L.C. Leal, K. Guber,D.      2831 1451
Wiarda, and G. Arbanas.                                           2831 1451
     The previous set of resonance parameters was based on the    2831 1451
SAMMY analysis of ORNL neutron transmission,  and capture         2831 1451
measurements by C.M Perey et al.(1).The present results were      2831 1451
obtained by adding to the SAMMY experimental data base the        2831 1451
capture cross sections recently measured at ORELA by K.H. Guber   2831 1451
et al.(2) and the GELINA very high resolution transmission        2831 1451
measurements performed by Brusegan et al.(3).  A complete         2831 1451
Resonance Parameter Covariance Matrix(RPCM) is obtained from      2831 1451
the SAMMY analysis of the experimental data base made consistent  2831 1451
by neutron energy scale adjustment, and normalization and         2831 1451
background corrections.  The RPCM contains mainly the             2831 1451
uncertainties and the correlations due to the statistical errors  2831 1451
of the experimental data base.  The systematic uncertainties of   2831 1451
the experimental data, estimated from preliminary SAMMY analyses  2831 1451
of the experimental data base, are taking into account in cross   2831 1451
section covariance files for the total, scattering and capture    2831 1451
cross sections.  Since the capture cross sections are very small  2831 1451
compared to the total cross sections the scattering cross         2831 1451
section covariance file has been taken identical to the total     2831 1451
cross section covariance file. Due to the fact that some          2831 1451
components of the systematic uncertainties are not fully          2831 1451
correlated (background, deviation between the theoritical and     2831 1451
expirimental data in the SAMMY fits etc...) all the non-diagonal  2831 1451
elements of the cross section correlation matrices were settled   2831 1451
arbitrarily at 0.5.                                               2831 1451
     The thermal cross sections calculated from the resonance     2831 1451
are the following, compared to the last Mughabghab(5)             2831 1451
evaluation and to ENDF/B-VII-0:                                   2831 1451
                                                                  2831 1451
                    Present         Mughabghab      ENDF/B=VII-0  2831 1451
     Total        3.50+-0.10  b     3.49+-0.09 b       3.90  b    2831 1451
     Capture      2.40+-0.06  b     2.50+-0.06 b       2.92  b    2831 1451
     Scattering   1.10+-0.03  b     0.98+-0.07 b       0.98  b    2831 1451
                                                                  2831 1451
The value of the capture cross section is a weighted average of   2831 1451
the experimental data.  Adjustment of the thermal capture cross   2831 1451
section could be obtained by small variation of the capture       2831 1451
width of the resonance at -150 eV.                                2831 1451
     The capture resonance integral calculated in the energy      2831 1451
range 0.5 eV to 800 keV is 1.259 b compared to 1.394 b from       2831 1451
ENDF/B-VII-0 and 1.4 b from the last Mughabghab evaluation.       2831 1451
     The direct capture component at 0.0253 eV was estimated to   2831 1451
be 0.51 b from a Direct-SemiDirect calculation by G. Arbanas(4).  2831 1451
The direct capture contribution is included in a file 3           2831 1451
background for the total and capture cross sections in the        2831 1451
energy range thermal to 812 keV.                                  2831 1451
                                                                  2831 1451
(1) C.M. Perey et al., ORNL/TM-5893.1982.                         2831 1451
(2) K.H. Guber, Private communication, 2008.                      2831 1451
(3) A. Brusegan et al., Int. Conf. Nucl. Data for Science and     2831 1451
    Technology, Gatlinburg May 1994, Vol.1, page 224.             2831 1451
(4) G. Arbanas, Private Communication, 2008.                      2831 1451
(5) S.F. Mughabghab,Atlas of Neutron Resonances, Elsevier, 2006   2831 1451
